The code statements that lead to the bug are:
unsafe { data.__pinned_init(drvdata_ptr.cast())? };
Reason:
After `pwmchip_alloc()` returns a pointer with an initial reference, if `__pinned_init()` fails, the `?` operator immediately propagates the error without calling `pwmchip_put()` to release the reference, causing a memory leak.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
unsafe { data.__pinned_init(drvdata_ptr.cast()) }.inspect_err(|_| {
    unsafe { bindings::pwmchip_put(c_chip_ptr) }
})?;
Reason:
The `.inspect_err()` closure is called only when `__pinned_init()` returns an error. Inside it, `pwmchip_put()` is called to release the reference obtained by `pwmchip_alloc()`, then the `?` operator propagates the error. This ensures no reference leak on failure.

